description:
AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6 (Cache-friendly result types) requires public result/error types to be Send+Sync with a compile-time assertion. pkix-chain/src/lib.rs only asserts Send+Sync for Error (lines 1609-1612):

    const _: fn() = || {
        fn _assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
        _assert_send_sync::<Error>();
    };

But Verifier<'a, V, R, A> is also public surface. The non-negotiable explicitly lists 'verify_batch' and prevalidation as 'must remain possible' patterns. verify_batch ALREADY exists on Verifier; concurrent batching (multiple chains across a thread pool) is a natural consumer. Without a Send+Sync guarantee, callers either avoid Verifier in concurrent setups (forcing them back to verify_chain free-function calls with per-call setup cost) or discover the missing bound during a refactor.

Verifier is Send+Sync iff &'a [TrustAnchor], &'a V, &'a R, &'a ValidationPolicy, &'a A are all Send+Sync. The refs are Send+Sync iff the underlying types are Sync. TrustAnchor and ValidationPolicy already derive trivially-Send-and-Sync data. The trait bounds on V, R, A don't require Sync — so the Verifier impl IS implicitly Send+Sync only for V/R/A that happen to be Sync.

SCENARIO that triggers the cost: a contributor adds a NewBuiltInFetcher to pkix-aia (e.g. with internal Cell<T> or RefCell<T> for connection pooling) that is NOT Sync. Verifier<'_, V, R, NewBuiltInFetcher> becomes !Sync silently. Anyone who tries to use it across rayon par_iter() or a tokio task gets a compile error pointing at the consumer, not at the offending fetcher. The error message blames the wrong layer.

MAINTENANCE CONSEQUENCE: a Send+Sync regression in any of V/R/A propagates silently to consumers. A const _ assertion in pkix-chain that names the bounds explicitly catches the regression at its source.

FIX: add an assertion like

    const _: fn() = || {
        fn _assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
        _assert_send_sync::<Verifier<'_, DefaultVerifier, NoRevocation, NoAiaFetcher>>();
    };

The concrete-type assertion is sufficient: if the default backends aren't Send+Sync, nothing else will be either; if they are, the generic Verifier becomes Send+Sync exactly when its parameters are. This is the same pattern already used for Error.

The CHANGELOG entry for c64dc840 ('chore: add Send+Sync compile-time assertions') claims this is done. It's done for Error only; the wider claim is incorrect.
